  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Saturday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Skies are sunny across northern and\ncentral Nevada this afternoon. Satellite imagery is showing\nplentiful smoke from area wildfires across much of the area. The\nsmoke is producing reduced visibility in spots along with reduced\nair quality. There is not much in the way of moisture and we will\nbe expecting another night of quiet weather with lows in the 50s.\n\nNot much change in the pattern is expected as we start the new\nwork week. Upper level ridging will be centered across Arizona\nwhile an upper trough continues its eastward progression over\nsouthern Canada. Afternoon breezes will once again develop\ntomorrow with gusts of 20 to 25 mph. It will be a little bit\ncooler with highs generally in the low to mid 90s. Smoke will be\nprevalent in a majority of locations due to the ongoing wildfires.\nIt will be a calm and cool overnight period with no precipitation\nexpected and lows in the 50s.\n\nTuesday through Thursday, the upper level ridge is expected to \nundergo a period of strengthening while moving slowly westward. \nThis will bring a slow warmup to the area. Though high \ntemperatures on Tuesday will be a few degrees warmer with \nreadings in the mid to upper 90s, by Wednesday and Thursday, highs\nwill once again climb back into the triple digits for many \nlocations. Heat related products may be required as HeatRisk\nindices climb to moderate to major. Here are some probabilities \nfor high temperatures greater than 100 degrees for select \nlocations across northern and central Nevada for Thursday:\n\nAustin \u2013 0%\nBattle Mountain \u2013 85% \nElko \u2013 80% \nEly \u2013 0% \nEureka \u2013 0% \nJackpot \u2013 25%\nJarbidge \u2013 0%\nOwyhee \u2013 55%\nRuth \u2013 0% \nSpring Creek \u2013 60% \nTonopah \u2013 0% \nWells \u2013 70% \nWest Wendover \u2013 75%\nWildhorse Res \u2013 5%\nWinnemucca \u2013 80%\n\nThere will be some afternoon breezes each day, but nothing too \nstrong is expected. Overnight lows each night will be in the 50s \nto even the 60s in the warmer locales.\n\nBy Friday and Saturday, all models are showing the upper ridge\ncirculation across southern Nevada and Utah. At this time, both\nThursday and Friday afternoons appear to be the warmest with\nFriday's readings in the upper 90s to mid 100s. A weak push of\nmid-level moisture may bring some very high-based cumulus to\nportions of central Nevada, along with an isolated dry\nthunderstorm to central Nevada. Afternoon breezes continue with\ngusts to 20 mph possible. Here are some probabilities \nfor high temperatures greater than 100 degrees for select \nlocations across northern and central Nevada for Friday:\n\nAustin \u2013 2%\nBattle Mountain \u2013 99% \nElko \u2013 95% \nEly \u2013 0% \nEureka \u2013 0% \nJackpot \u2013 35%\nJarbidge \u2013 0%\nOwyhee \u2013 65%\nRuth \u2013 0% \nSpring Creek \u2013 80% \nTonopah \u2013 0% \nWells \u2013 90% \nWest Wendover \u2013 95%\nWildhorse Res \u2013 5%\nWinnemucca \u2013 90%\n\nLook for dry conditions Friday night with lows in the 50s and \n60s. The upper ridge circulation will shift east for Saturday with\na continuation of the mid-level moisture creep, leading to a few \nmore isolated dry thunderstorms in central Nevada. Highs will be a\ndegree or two cooler, but will still be hot with readings in the \n90s and 100s. Overnight lows will be in the 50s and 60s.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: Confidence remains high with\nrespect to the upper ridge through the period. Moderate to high \nconfidence exists with temperatures warming back to the triple \ndigits by the middle portions of the week. Confidence is very low \non the possibility of isolated dry thunderstorms Friday and \nSaturday in central Nevada. Only minor changes to the wind fields \nwere made in the near term NBM forecast.",
